If your acquired land is cultivable and bears some profit, the compensation is calculated as five times the net annual profit from such lands, or ten times the annual raiyati rent for an equal area of cultivated land in the neighbourhood, whichever is greater (State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950, Section 39(1)(c)(i)).

The net annual profit is determined by:  
(a) calculating the gross value of the annual produce of the land as the product of its normal annual yield (determined in the prescribed manner) and the average price of each kind of produce during the ten years immediately preceding the year in which the assessment-roll is prepared;  
(b) deducting from this gross value:  
 (i) the cost of cultivation, not exceeding fifty per centum of the gross value;  
 (ii) the land revenue or rent and cesses payable annually by you in respect of the land;  
 (iii) taxes under the Bengal Agricultural Income-tax Act, 1944; and  
 (iv) taxes under the Income-tax Act, 1922 (State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950, Section 39(2)).

Under the Succession Act, 1925, a person who dies without making a testamentary disposition capable of taking effect is deemed to have died intestate in respect of such property (Section 30). The inheritance of such property follows the rules of intestate succession laid down in the Act, particularly in Schedules I and II, which prescribe the order of heirs based on religion and relationship.

For Hindus, Buddhists, Jains, and Sikhs, the distribution is governed by Part I of Schedule I. For Christians, Parsis, and others not covered by specific personal laws, the rules in Schedule II apply. For example:

- Under Section 54, if a Parsi dies intestate without lineal descendants but leaves a widow or widower, the property is divided as follows:  
  (a) If only a widow or widower survives, they take half;  
  (b) If both a widow/widower and a widow of a lineal descendant survive, the former takes one-third and the latter(s) share another one-third;  
  (c) If only widows of lineal descendants survive, they collectively receive two-thirds in equal shares;  
  (d) The residue is distributed among other relatives as per Part I of Schedule II.

In general, where no specific provisions apply, the estate devolves upon the nearest relatives according to the order of priority set out in Schedules I and II. Where there are no heirs under these schedules, the property may escheat to the government (as per general principles of succession law, though not explicitly stated in the provided excerpts).

