Yes, a neighbor can apply to become the guardian of a minor even if the child’s parents are not nearby.

Under Section 8 of the Guardians and Wards Act, 1890, an order for guardianship may be made only on the application of:  
(a) the person who wants to be the guardian;  
(b) any relative or friend of the minor;  
(c) the Collector of the district; or  
(d) the relevant authority for the class to which the minor belongs.  

A neighbor qualifies as a “friend” of the minor under clause (b), so they are legally allowed to file an application for guardianship.

However, the court will not appoint anyone as guardian unless it is satisfied that doing so is in the welfare of the child (Section 17(1)). The court considers factors like:  
- the age, sex and religion of the minor;  
- the character and capacity of the proposed guardian;  
- how close the proposed guardian is to the minor by kinship;  
- any wishes expressed by a deceased parent; and  
- any existing relationship between the proposed guardian and the child or their property (Section 17(2)).  

Also, if the minor is old enough to form an intelligent preference, the court may take that into account (Section 17(3)). And no one can be made guardian against their will (Section 17(5)).

So while a neighbor can apply, the final decision depends entirely on what the court believes is best for the child’s welfare — not just on who applied.

