Yes, police in Bangladesh can arrest someone without a warrant under certain conditions specified by law.

The main legal basis for this is Section 54 of The Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898. Under this section, a police officer may arrest without a warrant any person who:

- Has been involved in a cognizable offence, or there is a reasonable complaint, credible information, or suspicion against them;
- Is found with tools used for house-breaking and cannot prove a lawful excuse;
- Has been officially proclaimed as an offender;
- Is in possession of something reasonably suspected to be stolen property, and there is reason to believe they stole it;
- Obstructs a police officer while on duty, or tries to escape from legal custody;
- Is reasonably suspected of being a deserter from the armed forces of Bangladesh; or
- Has been involved in an act outside Bangladesh that would be a crime if done in Bangladesh, and is subject to extradition laws.

Also, under Section 151 of the same Code, a police officer may arrest someone without a warrant if they know of a plan to commit a cognizable offence and believe the crime can only be prevented by arresting the person planning it.

In addition, police officers in metropolitan areas like Dhaka, Chittagong, and Khulna have special powers under their respective Metropolitan Police Ordinances:

- Section 100 of the Dhaka Metropolitan Police Ordinance, 1976;
- Section 103 of the Chittagong Metropolitan Police Ordinance, 1978; and
- Section 104 of the Khulna Metropolitan Police Ordinance, 1985.

These sections allow any police officer to arrest without a warrant anyone committing an offence in their presence or within their view — whether under that Ordinance or any other law.

When someone is arrested without a warrant, the officer must take them before a Magistrate without unnecessary delay (Section 60), and may search them and keep items found on them (except normal clothing) if they cannot be released on bail (Section 51).

Courts have made it clear that these powers are not open to abuse. In *Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors* (8 SCOB [2016] AD 1), the Appellate Division held that arrests without warrant must strictly follow the conditions in Section 54 — they cannot be done on a police officer’s personal opinion alone, as this would violate personal liberty.

So yes, police can arrest without a warrant — but only when the law clearly allows it.

If your child breaks the conditions of an alternative solution (বিকল্প পন্থা) instead of proceeding to formal court proceedings, the following steps apply under the Children Act, 2013:

The probation officer (প্রবেশন কর্মকর্তা), upon becoming aware that your child or any responsible guardian has violated the conditions of the alternative solution, must immediately inform the Child Court or the Child Police Officer in writing (Section 48(4)).

Upon receiving such information, the Child Court or the Child Police Officer may take one or more of the following actions under Section 51:

- (ক) Issue a revised order with modified conditions of the same type;  
- (খ) Issue an arrest warrant for the child;  
- (গ) Send a written notice requiring the child to appear before the Child Court or police station;  
- (ঘ) Forward the case file to the State Prosecutor to initiate formal judicial proceedings against the child;  
- (ঙ) Order the child to be sent to a certified institution; or  
- (চ) Issue any other order under this Act.

Additionally, if the alternative solution involved a family conference (পারিবারিক সম্মেলন), and the conditions agreed upon in that conference are breached, the probation officer must also report this breach in writing to the Child Court or Child Police Officer (Section 49(4)).

The process is designed to be corrective rather than punitive, but failure to comply with the terms of the alternative solution may lead to formal judicial intervention. The Child Court retains discretion to choose the most appropriate response based on the child’s best interests and circumstances (Sections 48–51).

No formal trial begins automatically; the decision to proceed to court rests solely with the Child Court or Child Police Officer after reviewing the probation officer’s report (Section 51).

