সিলেট মহানগরী পুলিশ আইন, ২০০৯-এর অধীনে কোনো অধস্তন কর্মকর্তা দুর্নীতি (যেমন ঘুষ গ্রহণ) করলে, তার বিরুদ্ধে নিম্নলিখিত বিভাগীয় শাস্তি দেওয়া যায়:

### ১. **বিভাগীয় শাস্তি (Administrative Punishments)**  
ধারা ১২(১) অনুসারে, পুলিশ কমিশনার বা তার ক্ষমতাপ্রাপ্ত ঊর্ধ্বতন কর্মকর্তা যদি কোনো অধস্তন কর্মকর্তাকে **দুর্নীতি** (যেমন ঘুষ গ্রহণ বা প্রদান) বা **অসদাচরণ**, **কর্তব্যে অবহেলা** ইত্যাদির দায়ে দোষী সাব্যস্ত করেন, তাহলে তিনি লিখিত আদেশের মাধ্যমে নিম্নলিখিত যেকোনো এক বা একাধিক শাস্তি দিতে পারবেন:

- (ক) চাকুরী হইতে বরখাস্ত (Dismissal from Service);  
- (খ) চাকুরী হইতে অপসারণ (Removal from Service);  
- (গ) বাধ্যতামূলক অবসর (Compulsory Retirement);  
- (ঘ) পদাবনতি (Reduction in Rank);  
- (ঙ) পদোন্নতি বন্ধকরণ (Barring Promotion);  
- (চ) অনূর্ধ্ব এক বৎসরের জ্যেষ্ঠতা বাজেয়াপ্তকরণ (Forfeiture of Seniority for one year Maximum);  
- (ছ) অনূর্ধ্ব এক মাসের বেতন ও ভাতাদি বাজেয়াপ্তকরণ;  
- (জ) বেতন বৃদ্ধি বন্ধকরণ;  
- (ঝ) অনূর্ধ্ব এক মাসের বেতনের পরিমাণ টাকা জরিমানা;  
- (ঞ) অনূর্ধ্ব ত্রিশ দিনের জন্য কোয়ার্টার গার্ডে আটক রাখা;  
- (ট) অনূর্ধ্ব ত্রিশ দিনের জন্য পুলিশ লাইনে আটক রাখা এবং তৎসহ এক্সট্রা ড্রিল, এক্সট্রা গার্ড, ফ্যাটিগ বা অন্য ডিউটি;  
- (ঠ) তিরস্কার;  
- (ড) দৈনিক দুই ঘন্টা করিয়া অনূর্ধ্ব ১৪ দিনের জন্য শাস্তিস্বরূপ ড্রিল প্রদান।  

> **ব্যাখ্যা**: ধারা ১২-এর ব্যাখ্যায় "দুর্নীতি" বলতে সরকারী অফিসে ক্ষমতা বা পদের অপব্যবহার, **ঘুষ গ্রহণ ও প্রদান** কে বোঝানো হয়েছে (ধারা ১২-এর ব্যাখ্যা)।

### ২. **ঔপনিবেশিক/ফৌজদারি দণ্ড**  
ধারা ৫০ অনুসারে, যদি কোনো পুলিশ কর্মকর্তা **ভীরুতার অপরাধে** বা **ইচ্ছাকৃতভাবে আইন, বিধি বা আদেশ লঙ্ঘন** করে (যা দুর্নীতির অন্তর্ভুক্ত), তবে তাকে:  
- **ছয় মাস পর্যন্ত কারাদণ্ড**, বা  
- **এক হাজার টাকা পর্যন্ত অর্থদণ্ড**, বা  
- **উভয় দণ্ড** দেওয়া যাবে।  

> ঘুষ গ্রহণ একটি ইচ্ছাকৃত আইন লঙ্ঘন হিসেবে ধরা হয়, তাই ধারা ৫০-এর অধীনে ফৌজদারি দণ্ডও প্রযোজ্য।

### ৩. **অতিরিক্ত বিষয়: আয়ের অসঙ্গতি**  
ধারা ১২(১)-এর প্রাসঙ্গিক উপ-ধারায় উল্লেখ করা হয়েছে যে, **"জ্ঞাত আয়ের সাথে সঙ্গতিপূর্ণ নয় এমন আয়েশী জীবন"** দুর্নীতির অন্তর্ভুক্ত। যদি কর্মকর্তার আয় ও সম্পত্তি তার বেতনের সাথে অসামঞ্জস্যপূর্ণ হয়, তবে তা দুর্নীতির প্রমাণ হিসেবে ব্যবহার করা যেতে পারে।

### ৪. **প্রক্রিয়াগত শর্ত**  
- ধারা ১২(২) অনুযায়ী, এই বিভাগীয় শাস্তি পরিদর্শক ব্যতীত অন্য কোনো অধস্তন কর্মকর্তার বিরুদ্ধে **উপ-পুলিশ কমিশনারের অধস্তন নয় এমন ঊর্ধ্বতন কর্মকর্তা** দ্বারা প্রয়োগ করা যাবে।  
- ধারা ১২(৩) অনুসারে, পুলিশ কমিশনার বা তার ক্ষমতাপ্রাপ্ত ঊর্ধ্বতন কর্মকর্তা **সাময়িকভাবে** অধস্তন কর্মকর্তাকে বরখাস্তও করতে পারবেন।

### সারসংক্ষেপ:  
| শাস্তির ধরন | আইনের ধারা | বিস্তারিত |
|--------------|-------------|------------|
| **বিভাগীয় শাস্তি** | ধারা ১২(১) | বরখাস্ত, অপসারণ, পদাবনতি, বেতন কাটা, জরিমানা, ড্রিল, তিরস্কার ইত্যাদি |
| **ফৌজদারি দণ্ড** | ধারা ৫০ | ছয় মাস কারাদণ্ড বা ১,০০০ টাকা অর্থদণ্ড বা উভয় |
| **আয়ের অসঙ্গতি** | ধারা ১২(১) | জ্ঞাত আয়ের সাথে অসামঞ্জস্যপূর্ণ সম্পদ দুর্নীতির প্রমাণ হিসেবে গণ্য |

এই শাস্তিগুলো একসাথেও প্রয়োগ করা যেতে পারে — বিভাগীয় শাস্তি (প্রশাসনিক) এবং ফৌজদারি দণ্ড (আদালতি) একই ঘটনার জন্য পৃথকভাবে প্রয়োগ করা যায়।

According to the Jagannath University Act, 2005 (Act No. 28 of 2005), the Syndicate is the supreme executive body responsible for managing the operations and assets of Jagannath University.

Specifically:

- Section 19 states that the Syndicate shall be the executive body of the University and, subject to the provisions of this Act and the powers conferred on the Vice-Chancellor, shall have general management and superintendence over the affairs, institutions, and properties of the University. (Section 19)

- Section 3 also confirms that the University is a corporate body with perpetual succession and a common seal, and has the power to acquire, hold, and transfer both movable and immovable property, and to sue or be sued in its own name. However, the day-to-day management and oversight of these powers are vested in the Syndicate under Section 19.

- Additionally, Section 56(1)(ক) clarifies that upon the abolition of Jagannath Government College, all its movable and immovable properties, cash, bank deposits, claims, rights, liabilities, and debts shall become the property, rights, powers, authority, facilities, assets, funds, claims, rights, liabilities, and debts of the University — which are then managed by the Syndicate.

The functions of the Syndicate include:
- General management and supervision of university affairs and properties (Section 19);
- Acquisition, holding, control, and administration of university property and funds (analogous to Section 20(ঙ) in other universities’ acts);
- Approval of annual budgets and financial reports;
- Oversight of the common seal;
- Administration of special-purpose funds;
- Appointment of staff where no other provision exists; and
- Ensuring compliance with the Act, Statutes, Ordinances, and Regulations.

Thus, the Syndicate holds the primary legal authority to manage the operations and assets of Jagannath University under the Jagannath University Act, 2005 (Section 19).

For a Christian marriage in Bangladesh, the written notice must include the following information, depending on whether the marriage is to be solemnized by a Marriage Registrar or a licensed Minister of Religion:

### If the marriage is to be solemnized by a **Marriage Registrar** (under Section 38):
The notice must state:
- The **name and surname** of each party intending to marry;
- The **profession or condition** of each party;
- The **dwelling-place** of each party;
- The **time during which each has dwelt** in that place;
- The **place at which the marriage is to be solemnized**.

*Provided*: If either party has dwelled in the stated place for more than one month, it may be stated simply as “one month and upwards.”

### If the marriage is to be solemnized by a **Minister of Religion** licensed under the Act (under Section 12):
The notice must state:
- The **name and surname**, and **profession or condition**, of each party;
- The **dwelling-place** of each party;
- The **time during which each has dwelt** in that place;
- The **church or private dwelling** where the marriage is to be solemnized.

*Provided*: If either party has dwelled in the stated place for more than one month, it may be stated simply as “one month and upwards.”

### Additional Notes:
- The notice must be in writing and follow the form in the First Schedule of the Act or be to a similar effect (Sections 38 and 12).
- If either party is a Native Christian who does not understand English, the Marriage Registrar (Section 57) or Minister of Religion (Section 23) must translate the notice into a language the party understands, or otherwise ascertain that the party comprehends its purport and effect.
- The Marriage Registrar must file the notice in official records and enter a true copy into the “Marriage Notice Book,” which is open for public inspection without fee (Section 40).
- A copy of the notice must be affixed in a conspicuous place in the Registrar’s office (Section 39), and if a party is a minor, copies must also be sent to other Marriage Registrars in the district within 24 hours.

These requirements apply under **The Christian Marriage Act, 1872**.

If a marriage certificate has already been sent to the Registrar General of Births, Deaths and Marriages and an error is discovered, the person charged with the duty of registering the marriage may correct the error by making a marginal entry. This correction must be made:

- Within one month after the discovery of the error;
- In the presence of the persons married; or, if they are dead or absent, in the presence of two other credible witnesses;
- Without altering the original entry;
- By adding a marginal entry that clearly states the correction;
- The person making the correction must sign the marginal entry and add the date of the correction.

Additionally, this marginal entry must be attested by the witnesses present when it was made. 

Since the certificate has already been sent to the Registrar General, the same person who registered the marriage must also prepare and send a separate certificate that includes:
- The original erroneous entry; and
- The marginal correction made thereto.

This procedure is governed under Section 78 of the Christian Marriage Act, 1872.

বাংলা একাডেমির মহাপরিচালককে অর্থনৈতিকভাবে দেউলিয়া হওয়ার কারণে অপসারণ করা যাবে, যদি তিনি কোনো উপযুক্ত আদালত কর্তৃক দেউলিয়া ঘোষিত হন। এটি বাংলা একাডেমি আইন, ২০১৩-এর ধারা ২৮(২)(ক) অনুযায়ী স্পষ্টভাবে উল্লিখিত।

ধারা ২৮(২) অনুসারে, সরকার মহাপরিচালককে তাঁহার পদ হইতে অপসারণ করিতে পারিবে, যদি তিনি—  
(ক) কোনো উপযুক্ত আদালত কর্তৃক দেউলিয়া ঘোষিত হন;  

এই অবস্থায় নির্বাহী পরিষদের অনাস্থা জ্ঞাপন বা অন্য কোনো প্রক্রিয়ার প্রয়োজন হয় না; আদালতের দেউলিয়া ঘোষণা একটি স্বয়ংসিদ্ধ কারণ হিসাবে কাজ করে।

অন্যান্য অপসারণের কারণগুলি হল:  
- পারিশ্রমিকের বিনিময়ে স্বীয় দায়িত্ব-বহির্ভূত অন্য কোনো পদে নিয়োজিত হওয়া (খ);  
- কোনো উপযুক্ত আদালত কর্তৃক অপ্রকৃতস্থ ঘোষিত হওয়া (গ);  
- নৈতিক স্খলনজনিত কোনো ফৌজদারি অপরাধে দোষী সাব্যস্ত হওয়া (ঘ)।  

এছাড়া, মহাপরিচালকের নিয়োগ তিন বছরের জন্য এবং একই ব্যক্তি দুই মেয়াদের অধিক নিয়োগ পাইতে পারেন না (ধারা ২৬(৩))। তবে এটি অপসারণের কারণ নয়, শুধুমাত্র মেয়াদ সীমাবদ্ধতা।

অতএব, যদি মহাপরিচালক কোনো উপযুক্ত আদালত কর্তৃক দেউলিয়া ঘোষিত হন, তবে সরকার প্রত্যক্ষভাবে তাঁহাকে অপসারণ করিতে পারেন (বাংলা একাডেমি আইন, ২০১৩, ধারা ২৮(২)(ক))।

Yes, police in Bangladesh can arrest someone without a warrant under specific conditions laid down by law.

Under Section 54 of The Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, a police-officer may arrest without a warrant or Magistrate’s order any person who:

- Has been concerned in any cognizable offence, or against whom a reasonable complaint has been made, credible information received, or reasonable suspicion exists (Section 54(1)(i));
- Has in his possession, without lawful excuse (burden of proof lies on the person), any implement of house-breaking (Section 54(1)(ii));
- Has been proclaimed as an offender under this Code or by Government order (Section 54(1)(iii));
- Is found in possession of anything reasonably suspected to be stolen property, and is reasonably suspected of having committed an offence relating to it (Section 54(1)(iv));
- Obstructs a police-officer while executing duty, or has escaped or attempts to escape from lawful custody (Section 54(1)(v));
- Is reasonably suspected of being a deserter from the armed forces of Bangladesh (Section 54(1)(vi));
- Has been concerned in, or against whom there is reasonable complaint, information, or suspicion regarding an act committed outside Bangladesh which would be punishable if done in Bangladesh, and for which extradition applies (Section 54(1)(vii)).

Additionally, under Section 151 of the same Code, a police-officer may arrest without warrant any person who is designing to commit a cognizable offence, if it appears that the commission of the offence cannot be otherwise prevented.

Police officers may also arrest without warrant any person committing an offence in their presence or within their view, under Section 100 of The Dhaka Metropolitan Police Ordinance, 1976; Section 103 of The Chittagong Metropolitan Police Ordinance, 1978; and Section 104 of The Khulna Metropolitan Police Ordinance, 1985. These provisions apply within their respective metropolitan areas and do not restrict other powers conferred by law (Explanation to each section).

Furthermore, when a person is arrested without warrant under any provision, the officer must, without unnecessary delay, take or send the arrested person before a Magistrate having jurisdiction (Section 60, CrPC). The officer may also search the arrested person and seize articles found on them (other than necessary wearing apparel) if the arrest is made under circumstances where bail cannot be furnished (Section 51, CrPC).

It is important to note that these powers are not discretionary in the sense of being arbitrary. As held in Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors, 8 SCOB [2016] AD 1, paragraph 122: “It is however not a matter of caprice, limited only by the police officers’ own view... Their powers are strictly defined by the Code, and being an encroachment on the liberty of the subject, an arrest purporting to be under the section would be illegal unless the circumstances specified in the various clauses of the section exist.” 

Thus, while arrests without warrant are legally permitted under these conditions, they must strictly conform to the statutory requirements.

If your child breaks the conditions of an alternative solution (বিকল্প পন্থা) under the Children Act, 2013, the following legal consequences apply:

The probation officer (প্রবেশন কর্মকর্তা) is required to immediately inform the Child Court or the Child Police Officer in writing about the breach of conditions (Section 48(4)). Upon receiving such information, the Child Court or the Child Police Officer may, after verifying the facts as prescribed by law (Section 51), take any one or more of the following actions:

- Reissue the same order with modified conditions;  
- Issue a warrant for the arrest of the child;  
- Issue a written notice requiring the child to appear before the Child Court or police station;  
- Send the case file to the Public Prosecutor to initiate formal judicial proceedings against the child;  
- Order the placement of the child in a certified institution (প্রত্যয়িত প্রতিষ্ঠান); or  
- Issue any other order under the Children Act, 2013 (Section 51(ক) to (চ)).

Additionally, if the breach occurred following a family conference (পারিবারিক সম্মেলন), and the child or their guardian failed to comply with the agreed decision, the probation officer must also report this in writing to the Child Court or Child Police Officer (Section 49(4)).

The process is designed to be corrective rather than punitive, but non-compliance may lead to formal judicial proceedings if alternative measures fail. The child’s best interests remain the guiding principle throughout (Sections 48(1), 49(2), 51).

No formal trial can proceed unless the Child Court or Child Police Officer formally refers the matter for prosecution under Section 51(ঘ). Until then, the system prioritizes rehabilitation over punishment.

The Syndicate has the authority to manage the operations and assets of Jagannath University.

According to Section 19 of the Jagannath University Act, 2005 (Act No. 28 of 2005):

> “(1) The Syndicate shall be the executive body of the University, and, subject to the provisions of this Act and the powers conferred on the Vice-Chancellor, the Syndicate shall have the general management and superintendence over the affairs, concerns and properties of the University...”

Additionally, Section 20 of the Jamalpur Science and Technology University Act, 2017 (Act No. 24 of 2017), Section 17 of the Narayanganj Science and Technology University Act, 2023 (Act No. 60 of 2023), Section 20 of the Naogaon University Act, 2023 (Act No. 9 of 2023), and Section 20 of the Chandpur Science and Technology University Act, 2020 (Act No. 16 of 2020) contain substantially identical provisions, confirming that in Bangladeshi public universities established under modern legislation, the Syndicate is universally designated as the supreme executive body responsible for general management and superintendence over university affairs and properties.

The powers of the Syndicate include:
- General management and supervision of the University’s affairs and properties (Section 19);
- Acquisition, holding, control, and administration of the University’s property and funds (implied by consistent statutory language across Acts);
- Approval of annual budgets and financial statements;
- Determination of the form and custody of the University’s common seal;
- Oversight of compliance with the Act, Statutes, Ordinances, and Regulations;
- Administration of special-purpose funds;
- Recommendation or approval of amendments to Statutes;
- Appointment of teachers and staff where no other provision exists (Section 20 of Jamalpur, Narayanganj, Naogaon, and Chandpur Acts); and
- Acceptance of wills, donations, and transferred properties in favor of the University.

These powers are exercised subject to the provisions of the Jagannath University Act, 2005, and any powers specifically conferred upon the Vice-Chancellor. The Syndicate is thus the central governing body for operational and asset management under the law.

Yes, a neighbor may apply to become the guardian of a minor even if the child has no parents nearby, provided the application meets the procedural and substantive requirements under the Guardians and Wards Act, 1890.

Under Section 8 of the Act, an order for the appointment or declaration of a guardian shall not be made except on the application of:  
(a) the person desirous of being, or claiming to be, the guardian of the minor;  
(b) any relative or friend of the minor;  
(c) the Collector of the district or other local authority; or  
(d) the Collector having authority with respect to the class to which the minor belongs.  

A neighbor qualifies as a “friend” of the minor under Section 8(b), and thus has standing to file an application for guardianship (Section 8). The Act does not require the applicant to be a blood relative; friendship or close association sufficient to demonstrate concern for the minor’s welfare is adequate.

However, the Court shall appoint or declare a guardian only if it is satisfied that such appointment is for the welfare of the minor (Section 7(1)). In determining welfare, the Court must consider:  
- the age, sex and religion of the minor;  
- the character and capacity of the proposed guardian;  
- the nearness of kin to the minor;  
- the wishes of a deceased parent, if any;  
- any existing or previous relations between the proposed guardian and the minor or his property (Section 17(2));  
- and, if the minor is old enough to form an intelligent preference, that preference may be considered (Section 17(3)).  

Additionally, no person can be appointed guardian against their will (Section 17(5)), so the neighbor must consent to the appointment.

The application must also comply with procedural requirements under Section 10: it must be by petition signed and verified as per the Code of Civil Procedure, stating particulars such as the minor’s name, sex, religion, date of birth, ordinary residence, property (if any), custody arrangements, existing guardianship status, and the qualifications of the proposed guardian.

Jurisdiction lies with the District Court where the minor ordinarily resides for guardianship of the person (Section 9(1)), or where the minor has property for guardianship of property (Section 9(2)).

In sum:  
- A neighbor may apply under Section 8(b) as a “friend” of the minor;  
- The Court’s paramount consideration is the welfare of the minor (Sections 7 and 17);  
- Appointment requires consent of the proposed guardian (Section 17(5));  
- Proper petition must be filed with requisite details (Section 10).  

The absence of nearby parents does not disqualify a neighbor from applying, but it elevates the importance of demonstrating genuine care, suitability, and alignment with the minor’s welfare under Section 17.

Under the Bangladesh Labour Act, 2006 (Act No. 42 of 2006), if a establishment ordinarily operates for less than one hundred and eighty working days in a year, it is considered a seasonal establishment (Section 328). 

For such seasonal establishments, when restarting production after shutdown, the owner must notify the Chief Inspector in writing at least thirty (30) days before commencing operations again (Section 325(2)).

The notice must contain the following details:
- Name and location of the establishment;
- Name and address of the employer;
- Address for correspondence regarding the establishment;
- Nature of the work or business carried out in the establishment;
- Nature and quantity of energy used;
- Name of the manager of the establishment;
- Estimated number of workers; and
- Any other information prescribed by rules (Section 325(1), mutatis mutandis).

Thus, for a factory operating only 80 days per year and shutting down for months, the owner must notify the Chief Inspector at least thirty days prior to restarting production.

There is no provision in the provided excerpts that mandates deduction of tax at source when an educational or research institution in Bangladesh receives a donation or grant from abroad.

The excerpts detail tax deduction at source obligations for specific types of payments, such as:

- Fees for professional or technical services (Section 42 of the Income Tax Act, 2002; Section 21 of the Income Tax Act, 2004);
- Rental value of vacant land or plant/machinery (Section 20 of the Income Tax Act, 2009);
- Payments under local letters of credit (Section 18 of the Income Tax Act, 2018);
- Dividends (Section 7 of the Income Tax Act, 1991);
- Advertising payments to newspapers, magazines or TV channels (Section 20 of the Income Tax Act, 2009);
- Remittances from abroad for services rendered in Bangladesh or by a resident for a foreign person (Section 32 of the Income Tax Act, 2020).

None of these provisions apply to donations or grants received from abroad by educational or research institutions. Section 32 of the Income Tax Act, 2020, which covers remittances from abroad for services, revenue sharing, etc., does not include donations or grants as taxable events — and even if it did, it would only apply where the payment is for “services rendered in Bangladesh” or similar activities, not for general charitable or institutional support.

Additionally, while Section 32(1) of the Income Tax Act, 2020, imposes a 10% deduction on remittances for services, it provides an exception for amounts excluded from total income under paragraph 48 of Part A of the Sixth Schedule. Donations or grants received by eligible educational or research institutions may fall under such exemptions if they are specifically exempted under the Sixth Schedule — but no such exemption is cited in the provided excerpts.

No court decision in the provided material addresses tax deduction on foreign donations to educational or research institutions.

Therefore, based solely on the provided legal excerpts, **no tax is required to be deducted at source when an educational or research institution in Bangladesh receives a donation or grant from abroad**, unless such payment falls under another specific taxable category (e.g., if disguised as fees for services), which is not indicated here.

পৌরসভার কাউন্সিলরগণ নিম্নলিখিত সময়ে নির্বাচিত হইবেন, যথা:  

(ক) পৌরসভা প্রথমবার গঠনের ক্ষেত্রে, স্থানীয় সরকার (পৌরসভা) আইন, ২০০৯-এর ধারা ২০(২)(ক) অনুযায়ী, এই আইন বলবৎ হইবার পরবর্তী একশত আশি (১৮০) দিনের মধ্যে;  

(খ) পৌরসভার মেয়াদ শেষ হইবার ক্ষেত্রে, উক্ত মেয়াদ শেষ হইবার পূর্ববর্তী নব্বই (৯০) দিনের মধ্যে, ধারা ২০(২)(খ);  

(গ) পৌরসভা বাতিলের ক্ষেত্রে, বাতিলাদেশ জারির পরবর্তী একশত আশি (১৮০) দিনের মধ্যে, ধারা ২০(২)(গ);  

এছাড়াও, প্রত্যেক ওয়ার্ড হইতে একজন কাউন্সিলর নির্বাচিত হইবে, ধারা ২০(১); এবং মেয়র ও কাউন্সিলরগণ সরাসরি প্রাপ্তবয়স্কদের ভোটের মাধ্যমে নির্বাচিত হইবেন, ধারা ৬(৩)।  

পৌরসভার মেয়াদ প্রথম সভার তারিখ হইতে পাঁচ (৫) বছর পর্যন্ত থাকে, ধারা ৮(১), এবং পৌরসভা যথার্থভাবে গঠিত হইবে বলিয়া গণ্য হইবে, যদি মোট কাউন্সিলরগণের শতকরা পঁচাত্তর (৭৫%) নির্বাচন এবং মেয়র নির্বাচন সম্পন্ন হয়, ধারা ৮(২)।

Yes, police in Bangladesh may arrest a person without a warrant under several provisions of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898 and metropolitan police ordinances.

Under Section 54 of the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1898, any police-officer may, without an order from a Magistrate and without a warrant, arrest:

- Firstly, any person who has been concerned in any cognizable offence or against whom a reasonable complaint has been made or credible information has been received, or a reasonable suspicion exists of his having been so concerned;
- Secondly, any person having in his possession without lawful excuse (the burden of proving which excuse shall lie on such person) any implement of house-breaking;
- Thirdly, any person who has been proclaimed as an offender either under this Code or by order of the Government;
- Fourthly, any person in whose possession anything is found which may reasonably be suspected to be stolen property and who may reasonably be suspected of having committed an offence with reference to such thing;
- Fifthly, any person who obstructs a police-officer while in the execution of his duty, or who has escaped, or attempts to escape, from lawful custody;
- Sixthly, any person reasonably suspected of being a deserter from the armed forces of Bangladesh;
- Seventhly, any person who has been concerned in, or against whom a reasonable complaint has been made or credible information has been received or a reasonable suspicion exists of his having been concerned in, any act committed at any place out of Bangladesh, which, if committed in Bangladesh, would have been punishable as an offence, and for which he is, under any law relating to extradition or under the Fugitive Offenders Act.

Additionally, under Section 151 of the same Code, a police-officer knowing of a design to commit any cognizable offence may arrest, without orders from a Magistrate and without a warrant, the person so designing, if it appears to such officer that the commission of the offence cannot be otherwise prevented.

Furthermore, under Section 100 of the Dhaka Metropolitan Police Ordinance, 1976; Section 103 of the Chittagong Metropolitan Police Ordinance, 1978; and Section 104 of the Khulna Metropolitan Police Ordinance, 1985, any police-officer may arrest without warrant any person committing in his presence or within his views any offence punishable under the respective Ordinance or under any other law for the time being in force.

Section 51 also permits a search upon arrest without warrant where the person cannot furnish bail.

It is important to note that these powers are not discretionary in the sense of being arbitrary. As held in Bangladesh & ors Vs. BLAST & ors, 8 SCOB [2016] AD 1, paragraph 122: “This section gives the police wide powers of arresting persons without warrant. It is however not a matter of caprice, limited only by the police officers’ own view as to what persons they may arrest without warrant. Their powers are strictly defined by the Code, and being an encroachment on the liberty of the subject, an arrest purporting to be under the section would be illegal unless the circumstances specified in the various clauses of the section exist.”

Moreover, even where conditions for arrest without warrant exist, it remains discretionary whether to arrest or merely report to a Magistrate: “...notwithstanding the existence of the conditions specified in the section, it may be desirable in the circumstances of the particular case to simply make a report to the Magistrate instead of arresting the suspected persons.” (Ibid., paragraph 123).

Thus, police may lawfully arrest without warrant under these specific statutory conditions.
